# Skill: Find files in a directory

Use the helper `scripts/find_files.sh` to locate files under a directory tree.
It wraps `find` with sensible prunes (`.git/`, `build/`, `third-party/` skipped
by default) so results stay focused on source.

## How to run

```bash
# By extension (case-insensitive)
scripts/find_files.sh -e mlir <dir>

# By name glob
scripts/find_files.sh -n 'CMakeLists.txt' <dir>
scripts/find_files.sh -i '*lower*' <dir>      # case-insensitive glob
```

Key options: `-n/--name <glob>` (case-sensitive, repeatable),
`-i/--iname <glob>` (case-insensitive, repeatable), `-e/--ext <ext>`,
`-t/--type f|d|l` (default `f`), `-d/--maxdepth <N>`, `--size <spec>`
(e.g. `+1M`), `--newer-than '<when>'` (e.g. `'1 day ago'`),
`--exclude <glob>` (repeatable; `--exclude ''` disables default prunes),
`-0/--print0`, `--dry-run`.

## Agent guidance

- Default prunes skip `build/` and `third-party/`; pass `--exclude ''` first if
  you genuinely need to search those.
- Combine with xargs for bulk ops: `scripts/find_files.sh -0 -e py . | xargs -0 wc -l`.
- Use `-d/--maxdepth` to keep large trees fast when you only need top-level hits.
- For content search (not names), use the grep skill (`scripts/grep_search.sh`)
  instead.

## Examples

```bash
scripts/find_files.sh -e mlir bishengir/test
scripts/find_files.sh -n 'CMakeLists.txt' -d 3 .
scripts/find_files.sh -i '*pass*' --exclude '*/docs/*' bishengir/lib
```
